x = max(left_max, height[lo])\n                water += left_max - height[lo]\n                lo += 1\n            else:\n                right_max = max(right_max, height[hi])\n                water += right_max - height[hi]\n                hi -= 1\n        return water\n","approach":"**Two pointers with running maxima.** The water above cell `i` is `min(maxLeft(i), maxRight(i)) - height[i]`. Keep `left_max` and `right_max` for the parts already scanned from each end. If `height[lo] < height[hi]`, then `right_max >= height[hi] > height[lo]`, so the water level at `lo` is decided purely by `left_max`: add `left_max - height[lo]` and advance `lo`. Otherwise do the mirror step on `hi`.\n\n**Why it is correct:** the key invariant is that the side with the smaller current bar is bounded by a taller bar on the other side, so the smaller side's running max is the true `min` for that cell. Each cell is settled exactly once.\n\n**Common mistakes:** comparing `left_max` with `right_max` instead of `height[lo]` with `height[hi]` (both variants exist; be consistent and able to justify it); forgetting that the running max must include the current bar before subtracting (otherwise negative water); mixing this up with Container With Most Water.\n\n**Interview follow-ups:** Solve it with a monotonic stack (pop when a taller bar arrives, fill the pit between). Two-dimensional version, Trapping Rain Water II (min-heap from the border inward). If the heights arrive as a stream, what can you compute online? (only the left-max part; discuss limits).","complexity":{"time":"O(n)","space":"O(1)"},"similar":["container-with-most-water","product-of-array-except-self","largest-rectangle-in-histogram"],"number":22,"starter":"class Solution:\n    def trap(self, height: List[int]) -> int:\n        pass\n"}